<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Sisters</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as tres hermanas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three Sisters are squash, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and climbing beans, the three main crops of various Indigenous peoples of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, grown together by companion planting. The maize stalk serves as a trellis for the beans, the beans fix nitrogen and steady the maize in high winds, and the wide leaves of the squash shade the ground, keep the soil moist and hold back weeds. The crops and their polyculture originated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, where squash was domesticated first, about 10,000 years ago, followed by maize and then beans. Among the Haudenosaunee, where women grew and distributed them, the Three Sisters were the foundation of subsistence and appear in oral traditions and ceremonies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/three-sisters-agriculture.jpg" alt="Three Sisters" width="330" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anna Juchnowicz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Sisters_companion_planting_technique.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
